<section class="phero"><div class="phero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="641" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal process</li></ol></nav><p class="eyebrow on-dark">Process guide</p><h1>The Kentucky Insurance Appraisal Process, Step by Step</h1><p class="lede">Seven stages from the first disagreement to a signed award — what happens, who does what and where claims most often go wrong.</p><p class="btns"><a class="btn coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></p><ul class="chips"><li><small>Stages</small>7</li><li><small>Panel</small>2 appraisers + umpire</li><li><small>Binding</small>Any 2 of 3 sign</li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><ol class="stepper"><li><span>1</span><b>Confirm it is a pricing dispute</b></li><li><span>2</span><b>Send a written appraisal demand</b></li><li><span>3</span><b>Name your party appraiser</b></li><li><span>4</span><b>Select the umpire</b></li><li><span>5</span><b>Inspect and build estimates</b></li><li><span>6</span><b>Negotiate and narrow the differences</b></li><li><span>7</span><b>Sign the award and collect payment</b></li></ol><p>Appraisal is a way to settle a disagreement about the <strong>amount</strong> of a property loss without going to court. Most Kentucky homeowners, farm and commercial property policies contain an appraisal clause, and the process it describes is fairly consistent from one insurer to the next. This guide walks through each stage in plain English. Your own policy controls the details, so read your appraisal clause alongside it.</p>

<h2>Before you demand appraisal</h2>
<p>Appraisal works best when three things are true. First, the insurer has accepted that a covered loss happened, even if it disputes part of the scope. Second, the gap between the two numbers is large enough to justify the cost of appraisers and an umpire. Third, you have documented the loss well enough that someone else can price it.</p>
<p>Gather these before you send anything:</p>
<ul>
<li>A complete copy of the policy, including the declarations page and every endorsement.</li>
<li>The insurer’s estimate, any revised estimates and the payment letters.</li>
<li>Your contractor bids or an independent estimate, ideally in a line-item format.</li>
<li>Dated photos and video of the damage, plus receipts for emergency repairs and mitigation.</li>
<li>Your correspondence with the adjuster, including any reservation of rights letter.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are unsure whether your file is ready, our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/appraisal-readiness-checkup/">appraisal readiness checkup</a> walks through the common gaps. If the insurer has denied the claim outright or says the damage is excluded, that is a coverage dispute, and you should talk with a Kentucky attorney before relying on appraisal.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Confirm it is a pricing dispute</h2>
<p>Appraisal decides how much the loss costs, not whether the policy covers it. A typical appraisal-ready dispute sounds like “the adjuster priced a partial roof repair and our contractor says the whole slope needs replacement” or “the estimate leaves out overhead and profit and undervalues the drywall.” A dispute that sounds like “the insurer says wear and tear caused the leak” is usually about causation or coverage, and appraisers in many policies have limited authority there.</p>
<p>Some disputes mix both. In those cases the appraisers may be asked to price the loss in a way that lets a court or the parties apply coverage later, for example by listing disputed items separately. How that works depends on the policy and on Kentucky law, which is another reason to get legal advice when coverage is truly contested.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Send a written appraisal demand</h2>
<p>Most policies say either party may demand appraisal in writing if the two sides fail to agree on the amount of loss. The demand should be short and factual: the policy number, the claim number, the date of loss, a statement that you disagree on the amount of loss, a statement that you are invoking appraisal, and the name and contact details of your appraiser if you have chosen one.</p>
<p>Send it in a way you can prove, such as certified mail or email with a delivery confirmation, and copy your adjuster. Keep in mind that the policy’s suit limitation clause may keep running while appraisal is under way. Policies set that period, and Kentucky law limits how short it can be, so read the clause and talk to an attorney if the date is getting close.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Name your party appraiser</h2>
<p>Many policies give each side about 20 days after the demand to name an appraiser. The clause commonly requires that appraiser to be “competent and impartial.” Competent means they understand construction pricing, estimating software and the kind of damage involved. Impartial is debated, but at a minimum your appraiser should disclose any relationship with you, your contractor or the insurer, and should not have a stake in the outcome that the policy forbids.</p>
<p>You pay your own appraiser. Fee arrangements vary, and our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/party-appraiser-representation/">party appraiser representation</a> page explains how we approach engagement terms and disclosure.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Select the umpire</h2>
<p>Once both appraisers are named, they try to agree on an umpire, commonly within about 15 days under many policies. The umpire is a neutral who only steps in when the appraisers cannot agree. Good umpires have deep estimating knowledge, a reputation for fairness and no ties to either side.</p>
<p>If the appraisers cannot agree, most policies let either party ask a court of record in the county where the property is located to appoint one. That adds time and expense, so appraisers usually try hard to agree. For more on what a neutral does, see our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/neutral-umpire-services/">neutral umpire services</a> page.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Inspect and build estimates</h2>
<p>Each appraiser inspects the property, often together, and reviews the claim file. They measure, photograph and document the damage, then each prepares a line-item estimate. In Kentucky most estimates are written in Xactimate or a similar platform using regional price lists, but the price list is a starting point, not a rule. Local labor availability after a big storm, access problems, steep or high roofs, and code requirements can all move the number.</p>
<h3>What helps at this stage</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clear access to the property and to any removed materials you saved.</li>
<li>Samples of damaged shingles, siding or flooring when matching is an issue.</li>
<li>Contractor invoices for work already completed.</li>
<li>Notes on code items your local building official requires.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 6: Negotiate and narrow the differences</h2>
<p>The appraisers compare estimates line by line. Many items fall away quickly once both sides look at the same photos and measurements. What remains is often a short list: a roof replacement versus a repair, a quantity of drywall, a steep charge, an overhead and profit question or a contents valuation.</p>
<p>If the appraisers agree on everything, they sign an award and the umpire never gets involved. If not, they submit only the disputed items to the umpire, along with their supporting documents. The umpire reviews those items, may inspect the property and decides each one.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep it organized</b>An award is easier to apply when it is broken out by coverage (dwelling, other structures, contents) and shows both replacement cost and actual cash value. Ask your appraiser to format it that way.</div>

<h2>Step 7: Sign the award and collect payment</h2>
<p>A written award signed by any two of the three, either both appraisers or one appraiser and the umpire, sets the amount of loss. The insurer then applies the policy: it subtracts the deductible and any prior payments, holds back recoverable depreciation if the policy pays replacement cost only after repairs, and issues payment on the timeline the policy and Kentucky claims-handling rules require. Mortgage companies are often named on the check.</p>
<p>To see how an award might translate into a payment, try the <a href="/appraisal-calculators/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">appraisal payoff estimator</a>.</p>

<h2>Costs and who pays</h2>
<p>Most policies say each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire’s fees and any other appraisal expenses equally. Appraiser fees are commonly hourly, flat or, in some arrangements, a percentage of the award, and some policies or practices restrict contingency fees, so ask about this up front. Umpire fees are usually hourly and depend on how many items are disputed and whether an inspection is needed.</p>
<p>As an illustrative example only: if the insurer estimated $18,000 and your contractor estimated $34,000, a realistic award somewhere in between could easily cover the cost of appraisal. If the gap is $2,500, it probably will not.</p>

<h2>What appraisal can’t do</h2>
<ul>
<li>It does not decide whether a loss is covered or excluded.</li>
<li>It does not decide bad-faith claims or claims under Kentucky’s Unfair Claims Settlement Practices Act. Those belong with a Kentucky attorney, and you can also file a complaint with the Kentucky Department of Insurance in Frankfort.</li>
<li>It does not usually award interest, attorney fees or damages beyond the amount of loss.</li>
<li>It does not override policy limits, deductibles or conditions such as repair-before-payment for depreciation.</li>
</ul>
<p>We are not a law firm and not a public adjuster. We help Kentucky property owners and insurers with the pricing side of a dispute. If you want a second opinion on whether appraisal fits your claim, <a href="/contact-us/">contact us</a>.</p></article>
